BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:talks-273463@cambridge-events
DTSTAMP:20261123T074851Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261203T171500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261203T190000
SUMMARY:Financing Foreign Policy: UK Government–Bank Relations During the
  Development of the 1981–2 Sovereign Debt Crisis in Poland\, Romania and
  Hungary
LOCATION:Lightfoot Room - St John's College
URL:https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273463/
CATEGORIES:Politics,International,Policy
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Catherine Lefevre\, Glasgow\nCore Seminar in Economic 
 and Social History\nTopics: Politics\, International\, Policy\nDetails: ht
 tps://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273463/\nThis talk demonstrates how UK ba
 nks helped finance the UK’s foreign policy toward Central and Eastern Eu
 ropean (CEE) during the Cold War with the support and encouragement of the
  UK government. It examines the relationship between the UK government\, U
 K banks and the Bank of England during the development of the CEE sovereig
 n debt crisis in Poland\, Romania and Hungary and shows that UK banks play
 ed a key role in the development of East–West relations during the Cold 
 War through actively developing business ties with CEE countries which the
  UK government saw as an opportunity to further its foreign policy goals i
 n the region.
END:VEVENT
